California Lemon Law provides important protections for consumers dealing with defective vehicles. Recent legislative changes have added new procedural requirements for certain claims. For anyone researching the [best Lemon Law attorney in San Diego](/locations/lemon-law-lawyer-san-diego), understanding Assembly Bill 1755 (AB 1755) provides useful context before pursuing a vehicle repurchase or replacement.

Signed into law on September 29, 2024, AB 1755 introduced new procedures for qualifying Lemon Law actions. The law addresses pre-suit notice, manufacturer response periods, mediation, documentation, and case timelines. These requirements can affect how a claim proceeds from the initial demand through potential litigation.

What Is California AB 1755?

AB 1755 amended California’s procedures for certain actions seeking restitution or replacement of qualifying motor vehicles. The legislation applies to covered claims based on noncompliance with an applicable express warranty.

The law created Chapter 12 of Title 10 of Part 2 of the California Code of Civil Procedure. Several provisions became operative beginning in 2025, including the formal pre-suit notice procedure.

For consumers and attorneys, the changes create specific procedural steps that require careful attention.

The New Pre-Suit Notice Requirement

Beginning April 1, 2025, a consumer seeking civil penalties must provide written notice to the manufacturer at least 30 days before filing the action.

The notice must contain specific information, including:

- The consumer’s name

- The vehicle identification number (VIN)

- A brief summary of repair history and vehicle problems

- A demand for repurchase or replacement

The notice must be sent in writing through an approved manufacturer email address or by certified or registered mail with return receipt requested.

This requirement makes accurate documentation particularly valuable. Repair records, warranty information, purchase documents, and communications can help establish the details included in the notice.

How Manufacturer Response Periods Work

AB 1755 establishes a defined period for manufacturers to respond after receiving qualifying notice.

A manufacturer can make an offer for restitution or replacement within 30 days of receiving the notice. If the manufacturer makes a qualifying offer, the transaction must be completed within 60 days from the original notice date under the statutory procedure.

The California Department of Consumer Affairs also explains additional procedures established through subsequent legislation. These include manufacturer acknowledgments, payment requirements, and specific timelines for completing agreed restitution or replacement.

Vehicle Possession Also Has Requirements

Vehicle possession is another important consideration under Section 871.24.

A consumer generally must retain possession of the vehicle for at least 30 days after the manufacturer receives the written notice. If the manufacturer makes an offer for restitution or replacement, possession may need to continue for the full 60-day period described in the statute.

These requirements make timing relevant when evaluating the next step in a Lemon Law matter.

Mandatory Mediation Changes the Litigation Process

AB 1755 also establishes mediation requirements for covered civil actions. The statute requires the parties to schedule mediation within a specified period after the defendant’s responsive pleading.

The law generally stays discovery until mediation concludes, subject to specific disclosures and limited depositions. The statute also establishes deadlines for initial disclosures and mediation scheduling.

This structure encourages early evaluation of the dispute while giving both sides defined procedural obligations.

